NAME
       at - run commands once, at a later time (also atq, atrm, batch)

SYNOPSIS
       at [-m] [-M] [-q QUEUE] [-f FILE] TIME
       at -t [[CC]YY]MMDDhhmm
       at -l                      (the same as atq)
       at -d JOB...               (the same as atrm)
       at -c JOB
       atq [-q QUEUE]
       atrm JOB...
       batch

DESCRIPTION
       at reads shell commands from standard input (or from a file with -f)
       and runs them once at TIME. Type the commands, one per line, after the
       at> prompt, then press Ctrl+D on an empty line to finish. at answers
       with the job number and when it will run:

           job 3 at Mon Mar  2 14:00:00 2026

       The commands run later with /bin/sh, in the directory you were in and
       with your environment. Anything they print is mailed to you, so send
       output to a file if you want to keep it.

       Jobs are run by the atd service: it must be running (systemctl enable
       --now atd). The waiting jobs are kept in /var/spool/at.

       atq lists your waiting jobs (root sees everybody's): job number, time,
       queue and owner. atrm removes jobs by number. batch is like at with no
       time: it runs the commands as soon as the system is not busy.

   Writing TIME
       now + 5 minutes, now + 2 hours, now + 3 days
              A time from now. Units: minutes, hours, days, weeks.

       16:30, 4:30pm, 4pm
              Today at that time, or tomorrow if it has already passed.

       noon, midnight, teatime
              12:00, 00:00 and 16:00.

       16:30 tomorrow, noon tomorrow
              That time on the next day.

       10:00 2026-03-05
              A time on a given date.

OPTIONS
       -f FILE
              Read the commands from FILE instead of standard input.

       -t TIME
              Give the time in the touch style, [[CC]YY]MMDDhhmm.

       -q QUEUE
              Use a queue named by one letter (a is the default for at, b
              for batch).

       -l     List the waiting jobs (like atq).

       -d JOB Delete a job (like atrm).

       -c JOB Show the full script that will run for that job, including the
              environment at saved.

       -m     Mail the user when the job has finished, even with no output.

       -M     Never mail the user.

       -v     Show the time the job will run before reading the commands.

EXAMPLES
       Write the date into a file in five minutes:

           echo "date > /tmp/when.txt" | at now + 5 minutes

       Run a script from a file at 4 in the afternoon:

           at -f /root/backup.sh 16:00

       As student, schedule a job tomorrow at noon, typing the command:

           at noon tomorrow
           at> touch /home/student/hello
           at> (press Ctrl+D)

       List the jobs, look at job 2, then remove it:

           atq
           at -c 2
           atrm 2

FILES
       /etc/at.allow, /etc/at.deny
              Who may use at. If at.allow exists, only the users listed there
              may; otherwise everybody except those in at.deny. root always
              may.

       /var/spool/at
              The waiting jobs.

NOTES
       For something that must happen again and again, use cron or a
       systemd timer instead: at runs each job only once.

SEE ALSO
       crontab(1), atd(8), systemd.timer(5), sh(1)
